<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Proves the ignored-values wording WITHOUT Revit.

    The same source-linking pattern Heron.BindingNote.TestHost,
    Heron.StackGuard.TestHost and Heron.Banner.TestHost already use, and for
    the reason those project files state: HeronIgnoredValues.cs touches no
    Autodesk type - it compares two collections of names - so it is linked
    here BY SOURCE rather than through a project reference. Referencing
    Heron.Revit.Addin would drag in RevitAPI.dll and make the one testable
    piece untestable on any machine without Revit.

    Linking the source has the second property those files name too: there is
    exactly ONE copy of the rule. A second implementation written to test the
    first is how `binds:` came to be honoured by the Python half and ignored
    by the C# executor (FRAGMENT-ISSUES row 96).

    Why it exists at all: BindNeeds walked only the needs it expected, so a
    supplied name matching none of them was read by nothing and reported by
    nothing, and the caller was told the whole thing had been applied. The
    wording here is the ONLY thing that now breaks that silence, and a
    compiler cannot catch a regression in it.

        Run it with `dotnet run`, pointed at this folder, passing
        -p:RevitVersion=2024. (XML comments cannot carry a double
        hyphen, so the flag is spelled out rather than shown.)
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>$(HeronTfm)</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Heron.IgnoredValues.TestHost</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Heron.IgnoredValues.TestHost</AssemblyName>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="..\..\revit\Heron.Revit.Addin\HeronIgnoredValues.cs"
             Link="HeronIgnoredValues.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
